{"id":"panther-tank","slug":"panther-tank","name":"Panzerkampfwagen V Panther","aliases":["VK3002","VK 3002","Panzer 5","Panzer V","PzKpfw V","Panther V","Panzer V-IV","Panther-M10","Sd.Kfz. 171","Panther (tank)","Panzer V Panther","PzKpfw V Panther","Pz.Kpfw.V Panther","Panzerkampfwagen V","Panther medium tank","Panther tank"],"kind":"vehicle","category":"tanks","subcategory":"German medium tank of WWII","description":"The Panther tank, officially Panzerkampfwagen V Panther with ordnance inventory designation: Sd.Kfz. 171, is a German medium tank of World War II. It was used in most European theatres of World War II from mid-1943 to the end of the war in May 1945.","history":"Panthers were supplied to form Panzer Abteilung 51 (Tank Battalion 51) on 9 January, and then Panzer Abteilung 52 on 6 February 1943.","development":"The weight of the production model was increased to 45 tonnes from the original plans for a 35-tonne tank. Hitler was briefed thoroughly on the comparison between the MAN and DB designs in the report by Guderian's tank commission. Armour protection appeared to be inadequate, while \"the motor mounted on the rear appeared to him correct\". He agreed that the \"decisive factor was the possibility of quickly getting the tank into production\". On 15 May 1942, Sebastian Fichtner informed MAN that Hitler had decided in favour of the MAN design for the Panther and ordered series production of the tank. The upper glacis plate was to be increased from 60 mm (2.4 in) to 80 mm (3.1 in). Hitler demanded that an increase to 100 mm (3.9 in) should be attempted and that at least all vertical surfaces were to be 100 mm (3.9 in); the turret front plate was increased from 80 mm (3.1 in) to 100 mm (3.9 in).\nThe Panther was rushed into combat before all of its teething problems had been corrected. Reliability was considerably improved over time, and the Panther proved to be a very effective fighting vehicle, although some of its design flaws, such as weak final drive, were never fully rectified.\nGeneral Hasso von Manteuffel considered the Panther Germany's \"most satisfactory\" tank, saying it \"would have been close to the ideal, had it been possible to design with a lower silhouette.\"","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Armament","items":[{"label":"Main Armament","value":"1 × 7.5 cm KwK 42, 75 mm KwK 42 L/70 tank gun with 79 rounds"},{"label":"Secondary Armament","value":"2 × 7,92 x 57 mm, 7.92 mm MG 34 machine guns with 5,100 rounds"}]},{"group":"Protection","items":[{"label":"Armour","value":"16–100 mm"}]},{"group":"Mobility","items":[{"label":"Engine","value":"V12 Maybach HL230 P30 petrol engine"},{"label":"Engine Power","value":"700 PS (690 hp, 515 kW)"},{"label":"Power/Weight","value":"15.39 PS (11.5 kW/tonne, 13.77 hp/ton)"},{"label":"Transmission","value":"ZF AK 7-200. (7 forward 1 reverse)"},{"label":"Suspension","value":"double torsion bar, interleaved road wheels"},{"label":"Speed","value":"55 km/h (early models), 46 km/h (later models)","numeric":55,"unit":"km/h"},{"label":"Operational Range (Road)","value":"260 km, Cross-country: 100 km","numeric":100,"unit":"km"},{"label":"Fuel Capacity","value":"730 L","numeric":730,"unit":"L"}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Weight","value":"44.8 t","numeric":44.8,"unit":"t"},{"label":"Length","value":"6.87 m, 8.66 m (with gun forward)","numeric":6.87,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Width","value":"3.27 m, 3.42 m (with skirts)","numeric":3.27,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Height","value":"2.99 m","numeric":2.99,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Crew","value":"5 (driver, radio operator/hull machine gunner, commander, gunner, loader)"}]},{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Designer","value":"MAN AG"},{"label":"Designed","value":"1942"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"MAN, Daimler-Benz, Maschinenfabrik Niedersachsen-Hannover"},{"label":"Produced","value":"1943–1945 (1946–49 postwar for the British Army)"},{"label":"Number Built","value":"~6,000"},{"label":"Unit Cost","value":"117,100 (without weapons, optics, or radio)"},{"label":"Unit Cost","value":"143,912 (combat ready)"},{"label":"Unit Cost","value":"2,000 Man hours"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"Nazi Germany"},{"label":"In Service","value":"1943–1945 (Nazi Germany)"},{"label":"In Service","value":"1944–1945 (Kingdom of Hungary)"},{"label":"In Service","value":"1944–1952 (France)"}]}],"specIndex":{"Main Armament":"1 × 7.5 cm KwK 42, 75 mm KwK 42 L/70 tank gun with 79 rounds","Secondary Armament":"2 × 7,92 x 57 mm, 7.92 mm MG 34 machine guns with 5,100 rounds","Armour":"16–100 mm","Engine":"V12 Maybach HL230 P30 petrol engine","Engine Power":"700 PS (690 hp, 515 kW)","Power/Weight":"15.39 PS (11.5 kW/tonne, 13.77 hp/ton)","Transmission":"ZF AK 7-200. (7 forward 1 reverse)","Suspension":"double torsion bar, interleaved road wheels","Speed":"55 km/h (early models), 46 km/h (later models)","Operational Range":"260 km, Cross-country: 100 km","Fuel Capacity":"730 L","Weight":"44.8 t","Length":"6.87 m, 8.66 m (with gun forward)","Width":"3.27 m, 3.42 m (with skirts)","Height":"2.99 m","Crew":"5 (driver, radio operator/hull machine gunner, commander, gunner, loader)","Designer":"MAN AG","Designed":"1942","Manufacturer":"MAN, Daimler-Benz, Maschinenfabrik Niedersachsen-Hannover","Produced":"1943–1945 (1946–49 postwar for the British Army)","Number Built":"~6,000","Unit Cost":"117,100 (without weapons, optics, or radio)","Origin":"Nazi Germany","In Service":"1943–1945 (Nazi Germany)"},"images":{"hero":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H26258%2C_Panzer_V_%22Panther%22.jpg","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H26258,_Panzer_V_%22Panther%22.jpg","width":799,"height":539,"license":"CC BY-SA 3.0 de","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/de/deed.en","attribution":"Unknown authorUnknown author"},"thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Bundes